{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/comments/6008296383","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/97#issuecomment-6008296383","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/97","id":6008296383,"node_id":"IC_kwDOTfIvP88AAAABZh9Tvw","user":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?u=54040c0a76ab1ab88b7fa34cf7f825d4789c0d6f&v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-06T02:47:27Z","updated_at":"2026-10-06T02:47:27Z","author_association":"OWNER","body":"<!-- pr97-novelty-disposition-20261006 -->\n\nClosing without merging or publishing a preprint: **no novel research contribution has been established**.\n\nThe ordinary smooth conditional tightness theorem is a classical corollary of Dathe–Khoule's 2012 affine-deformation criterion (Definition 2.1/Theorem 2.2, pp.102–103), the Eliashberg–Thurston tightness neighborhood theorem, and finite smooth Gray stability. Under `d alpha = alpha wedge omega`, the mixed coefficient is zero, so the prior criterion gives the same contact-plane pencil; a finite endpoint near the taut foliation is tight, and Gray stability transfers tightness to the given smooth contact form. The finite-interval approximation for C1 alpha is a routine regularization of this argument.\n\nOur longer supplement provides explicit C1-form/C2-disk smoothing details and verification artifacts. Those assertions were checked, but their historical novelty has not been established. They do not provide a verified novel resolution warranting publication under this project's research goal.\n\nThis is a novelty-based closure, not a finding that the corrected mathematics is invalid. The ordinary smooth conditional result is already implied by established results. We have **not** authenticated an earlier literally printed exact answer to Calegari Question 13.2 or a historical resolution covering every interpretation of the original question. The final 2003 chapter and other identified literature gaps remain unread. For that reason, a categorical global `already_solved` historical classification is not asserted. Question 13.1's existence/weak-sign problem remains outside this result.\n\nThe corrected proofs, mathematical and priority audits, two whole-package review rounds, and the fresh independent novelty-disposition check are retained in the repository's `draft_pr_publication_program_20260930/audits/pr97_10300055` research folder. The prepared note stays unpublished. No Zenodo DOI or publication-tracker row is created for this closure.\n\nSources: [Dathe–Khoule 2012, author-uploaded article transcription](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258228980_Sur_les_deformations_d%27un_feuilletage_de_codimension_1_en_structures_de_contact); [2025 coauthor reproduction, Theorem 6.2 and C1 attribution](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.00454v1); [Vogel 2011, p.42](https://doi.org/10.2140/gt.2011.15.41); [Dathe–Rukimbira 2008, Propositions 3.3–3.4](https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.3389v1); [Vogel 2016, Theorem 2.9](https://doi.org/10.2140/gt.2016.20.2439); [Calegari's actual 2002 author version, p.29](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0209081v1).\n\nAI tools were used extensively in these audits. The work has not undergone conventional human peer review or refereeing.\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/comments/6008296383/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null,"pin":null}